      "writtenOn": "2024-07-19T19:28:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "John, can you review the SkSLPipelineStageCodeGenerator callback changes in PS4-\u003ePS5?\n\nRob and James reviewed the rest of the graphite changes, but I had missed a case where runtime effects could declare functions that referenced their own uniforms. This lets the graphite shader code gen add the SSBO struct parameter to those functions so that we can get rid of the global index variable.",
      "revId": "1d4f775b097ff1e19e1b8ee350443365c67bde7e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7715f6f6_ee4247c6",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-19T19:52:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is there any reason that we can\u0027t just copy the uniform data to a global, instead of putting it on the stack and then passing it around across all the functions?\n\nPassing a function parameter around is technically a copy that we are hoping the compiler might optimize away, but there\u0027s a real risk that the compiler fails to eliminate the copy. Using a global eliminates that risk.\n\nIf this were Metal or something we could pass a reference but GLSL is purely pass-by-copy.",
      "revId": "1d4f775b097ff1e19e1b8ee350443365c67bde7e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "06cbb2f0_577e0194",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-19T19:58:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I will test the performance with holding the struct as a global. It does have a lot of simplicity to it, although I am averse to global variables (although the reason for that are unfounded, so...) In practice, most of these function calls are inlined away entirely at the sksl level, but I had been thinking about having the MSL generator use references to ensure we don\u0027t have a copy.\n\nI am not concerned about GLSL in this case because if/when Graphite emits glsl it will not be using storage buffers to aggregate uniform data so will not trigger this specific mode.",
      "parentUuid": "7715f6f6_ee4247c6",
      "revId": "1d4f775b097ff1e19e1b8ee350443365c67bde7e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f52065f2_bce5e4ec",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-19T20:00:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am averse to global variables for human-authored code. But at the compiler level, we don\u0027t need to worry that the code generator will get mixed up and put in the wrong name, or worry that we will clutter the namespace. I\u0027m also hesitant to make the pipeline-stage callbacks more complex. I think it\u0027d be worth trying a global?",
      "parentUuid": "06cbb2f0_577e0194",
      "revId": "1d4f775b097ff1e19e1b8ee350443365c67bde7e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7ed11945_09d21808",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 8341
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-19T20:25:33Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I tested it out and saw now perf difference. Ironically, the sksl-\u003emsl codegeneration defines a `Globals` struct and all global variables become fields of a stack-allocated instance of that; any reference to a global then has its signature augmented to include a reference to that struct.\n\nSo it ends up being equivalent to what I was trying to do here at the sksl level but guarantees that we are passing a reference around to the stack variable. The main benefit of then keeping a global `uniforms` value in this code gen is that the callbacks remain simple and we aren\u0027t changing any signatures to include extra parameters.",
      "parentUuid": "f52065f2_bce5e4ec",
      "revId": "1d4f775b097ff1e19e1b8ee350443365c67bde7e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e0a22c16_f2f4e047",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 11059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-07-19T20:32:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We pass around the globals by reference, not by copy, so even if the compiler can\u0027t figure out how to avoid the copy, it\u0027s still efficient. If we are passing around a copy of the entire uniforms struct and the compiler can\u0027t eliminate the copy for whatever reason, performance falls off a cliff.\n\nSince you\u0027ve already managed to code it up and it works at the same level of performance, would you be OK with doing it that way?",
      "parentUuid": "7ed11945_09d21808",
      "revId": "1d4f775b097ff1e19e1b8ee350443365c67bde7e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    }
  ]
